      "id": "exam/edexcel-9fm0-01-sample/q6",
      "question": "<div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\mathrm f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{x^2+9}\" data-display=\"true\"></div><ol class=\"parts\"><li>Show that<div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int\\mathrm f(x)\\,\\mathrm dx=A\\ln(x^2+9)+B\\arctan\\left(\\frac x3\\right)+c\" data-display=\"true\"></div>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"c\"></span> is an arbitrary constant and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B\"></span> are constants to be found. <span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></li><li>Hence show that the mean value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm f(x)\"></span> over the interval <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"[0, 3]\"></span> is<div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\frac16\\ln2+\\frac1{18}\\pi\" data-display=\"true\"></div><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></li><li>Use the answer to part (b) to find the mean value, over the interval <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"[0, 3]\"></span>, of<div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\mathrm f(x)+\\ln k\" data-display=\"true\"></div>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\"></span> is a positive constant, giving your answer in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p+\\dfrac16\\ln q\"></span>, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q\"></span> are constants and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q\"></span> is in terms of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\"></span>. <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></li></ol>",
